# Line-ending and whitespace policy.
#
# The Fortran rules below exist because fixed-form Fortran is column-sensitive:
# columns 1-5 are the statement label, column 6 marks a continuation, and the
# statement itself lives in columns 7-72. Anything that shifts characters
# sideways changes the meaning of the program, and does so invisibly -- the
# diff looks like whitespace, the compiler sees different code.
#
# Two distinct protections are at work, and they do different jobs:
#
#   text eol=lf
#       Pins the bytes. The repository stores LF, and every checkout gets LF,
#       so a contributor with core.autocrlf=true cannot silently rewrite line
#       endings on the way in or out. (gfortran itself tolerates CRLF; the
#       point is that the stored bytes stay identical to upstream's.)
#
#   whitespace=-trailing-space,-tab-in-indent
#       Tells git NOT to treat trailing whitespace or leading tabs as errors
#       in these files. This is the load-bearing one: without it,
#       `git apply --whitespace=fix` and `git rebase --whitespace=fix` will
#       happily strip trailing spaces and expand indent tabs, which is exactly
#       the corruption described above. It also silences the false positives
#       `git diff --check` would otherwise report on every Fortran hunk.

# Fixed-form Fortran (.f, .for, .f77) and free-form (.f90). Uppercase variants
# conventionally mean "run the C preprocessor first" and are just as sensitive.
*.f       text eol=lf whitespace=-trailing-space,-tab-in-indent
*.F       text eol=lf whitespace=-trailing-space,-tab-in-indent
*.for     text eol=lf whitespace=-trailing-space,-tab-in-indent
*.f77     text eol=lf whitespace=-trailing-space,-tab-in-indent
*.f90     text eol=lf whitespace=-trailing-space,-tab-in-indent
*.F90     text eol=lf whitespace=-trailing-space,-tab-in-indent

# f2py signature files describe the Fortran interface; keep them byte-stable too.
*.pyf     text eol=lf

# R sources vendored from peakfqr, read as the call-convention reference.
*.R       text eol=lf

# Make requires a literal TAB to open a recipe line. Expanding those tabs
# breaks the build outright, so the same whitespace exemption applies.
Makevars      text eol=lf whitespace=-trailing-space,-tab-in-indent
Makevars.win  text eol=lf whitespace=-trailing-space,-tab-in-indent

# Reference test data is byte-exact expected output from the USGS package.
# -text disables all normalization so the bytes match upstream exactly. The
# trade-off is that git treats these as binary and will not render textual
# diffs for them -- acceptable for fixtures that are never meant to change.
vendor/peakfqr/inst/testdata/**  -text
